{"study_accession":"PRJNA417194","secondary_study_accession":"SRP124159","title":"Precursors of human CD4+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes identified by single-cell transcriptome analysis [bulk RNA-seq]","description":"CD4+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes (CD4-CTLs) have been reported to play a protective role in several viral infections. However, little is known in humans about the biology of CD4-CTL generation, their functional properties, heterogeneity and clonal diversity, especially in relation to other well-described CD4+ memory T cell subsets. We performed single-cell RNA-seq in over 9000 cells to unravel CD4-CTL heterogeneity, transcriptional profile and clonality in humans. The single-cell differential gene expression analysis, revealed a spectrum of known transcripts, including several linked to cytotoxic and co-stimulatory function, and transcripts of unknown cytotoxicity-related function that are expressed at higher levels in the TEMRA subset, which is highly enriched for CD4-CTLs, compared to cells in the central and effector memory subsets (TCM, TEM). Simultaneous T cells antigen receptor (TCR) analysis in single-cells and bulk subsets revealed that CD4-TEMRA cells show marked clonal expansion compared to TCM and TEM cells and that the majority of CD4-TEMRA were dengue virus (DENV)-specific in subjects with previous DENV infection. The profile of CD4-TEMRA was highly heterogeneous across subjects, with four distinct clusters identified by the single-cell analysis. Most importantly, we identified distinct clusters of CD4-CTL effector and precursor cells in the TEMRA subset; the precursor cells shared TCR clonotypes with CD4-CTL effectors and were distinguished by high expression of the interleukin-7 receptor. Our identification of a CD4-CTL precursor population may allow further investigation of how CD4-CTLs arise in humans and thus could provide insights into the mechanisms that may be utilized to generate durable and effective CD4-CTL immunity. Overall design: Transcriptome analysis of purified populations of human CD4 memory cell subsets by bulk RNA-sequencing.","pubmed":"29352091","geo_series":"","n_samples":40,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN07980342","study_accession":"PRJNA417194","blood_class":"pbmc","class_from":"sample","title":"Bulk_RNA-seq_Subject16_V2_TEM","description":"Bulk_RNA-seq_Subject16_V2_TEM","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM2842505","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2018-01-25","attributes":[["source_name","Subject16_Visit 2_PBMC_TEM"],["longitudinal visit","Visit 2"],["cell_type","PBMC"],["cell subtype surface markers","CD3+CD4+CD8α/CD14/CD19-CD45RA-CCR7-"],["cell_subtype","TEM"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR6260148","experiment_accession":"SRX3366594","library_layout":"SINGLE","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 2500","read_count":19695803,"base_count":984790150,"first_public":"2018-01-25","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 2500 sequencing: GSM2842505: Bulk_RNA-seq_Subject16_V2_TEM Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":true},{"sample_accession":"SAMN07980343","study_accession":"PRJNA417194","blood_class":"pbmc","class_from":"sample","title":"Bulk_RNA-seq_Subject16_V2_TCM","description":"Bulk_RNA-seq_Subject16_V2_TCM","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM2842504","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2018-01-25","attributes":[["source_name","Subject16_Visit 2_PBMC_TCM"],["longitudinal visit","Visit 2"],["cell_type","PBMC"],["cell subtype surface markers","CD3+CD4+CD8α/CD14/CD19-CD45RA-CCR7+"],["cell_subtype","TCM"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR6260147","experiment_accession":"SRX3366593","library_layout":"SINGLE","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 2500","read_count":21849325,"base_count":1092466250,"first_public":"2018-01-25","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 2500 sequencing: GSM2842504: Bulk_RNA-seq_Subject16_V2_TCM Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":true},{"sample_accession":"SAMN07980344","study_acce